WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:02.650
I'm in the small village of Jelling, in Denmark.

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:05.150
And behind me, in that central churchyard,

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:06.500
are the Jelling Stones.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.180
They're runestones,

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:11.670
historical markers placed there by some of
the first kings of Denmark

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.250
more than a thousand years ago.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:15.690
Once they were painted bright colours,

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.839
but a millennium of erosion has taken its
toll.

00:00:18.839 --> 00:00:21.859
That's also why they're now in a climate-controlled
glass box

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:25.150
to keep them safe from the weather and from
vandals.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:28.300
The smaller, older stone was placed by King
Gorm

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.519
in honour of his wife, Thyra, but the larger
one:

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:33.989
that was placed by their son, whose name,

00:00:33.989 --> 00:00:38.269
once you translate it to English, was Harald
Bluetooth.

00:00:38.269 --> 00:00:41.050
The inscription honours his parents, and then
it says:

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:46.030
"Harald, who won for himself all of Denmark
and Norway and made the Danes Christian".

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.800
There's an inscription of Jesus on the back.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.880
Now, obviously, history is messy and complicated,

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.300
and if you are raising a memorial runestone
to yourself and to your own achievements,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.399
then you gloss over some of the less successful
bits of your history, but still:

00:01:02.399 --> 00:01:05.330
this is an incredibly important artifact.

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:07.500
It's called Denmark's birth certificate,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.710
it's part of this World Heritage Site,

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:14.650
and it tells the tale of a king who united
Denmark and Norway.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:19.570
A thousand years later, an engineer at Intel
called Jim Kardach

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:22.590
was working on short-range radio technology,

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:25.870
the sort of thing that might unite computers
and cell phones,

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:29.990
and make all those devices speak one compatible
protocol.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.110
The various names proposed for the various
technologies

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:37.090
were things like Biz-RF, MC-Link and Low Power
RF,

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:39.850
which were all a bit unwieldy.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:41.880
Kardack heard the story of Harald Bluetooth

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.340
from a Swedish person that he was working
alongside,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.720
and he figured, huh, that'd make a good codename
for the technology.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.490
And that's all it was.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:51.740
A codename.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.360
The official name that was decided later was
Personal Area Networking, or PAN,

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.180
a name that stuck until three weeks before
launch

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:04.030
when the lawyers said that they couldn't possibly
trademark something so generic.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:06.170
With no other options, they went with Bluetooth.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:07.840
And that logo?

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.009
Harald Bluetooth.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:12.970
HB in the runes of the time, more or less.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:16.100
Combine those two characters into what's called
a "bind rune",

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.880
sort of kind of like a signature...

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.220
and there's the Bluetooth logo.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.730
A thousand years ago, a Danish king stood
somewhere near here

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:27.110
and ordered those stones to be carved and
raised.

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:29.600
And now, his name is on our phones.

